s vibe succinctly so  here's just some of the novels that are  direct retellings of her work this  doesn't even begin to touch on film  comic book and television reiterations  of her work in which we have an  eight-hour long episode and this is a  publicly funded program so we're not  trying to waste your money with that  although go watch pbs's adaptation of  sandington arguably the most financially  successful from this list is helen  fielding's bridget jones diary and its  sequel the edge of reason based on  austin's novels pride and prejudice and  persuasion though the bridget jones  character existed before the publication  of both books as a recurring fictional  columnist for the independent when  writing her book fielding saw the  parallels between jones obsession with  modern femininity fashion and dating and  the way austin's elizabeth bennett finds  herself constantly struggling with the  social mores of regency england said  fielding rather cheekily when asked if  she lifted the plot of her books from  austin quote i thought both plot and  leading man had been well-marked  researched over a number of centuries  and that jane austen wouldn't mind  anyways she's dead austin wrote about  the minutia of women's lives in a way  that is funny and dazzlingly accurate  giving you insight into what was going  on in the social world without you're  even realizing you're getting an  incredible history lesson but we reach a  conundrum here despite bridget jones's  diary's success there is an issue  for all of the austin retellings that  exist in the market almost all of them  are relegated to the dreaded genre of  chicklet or women's literature  because we don't just read  why is it that if we can peg down  shakespeare and james joyce as  artistically acceptable examples of  literary recyclists that we look at  books like britta jones's diary or jenny  lee's best-selling retelling of anna  karenina anna k as disposable or  barely a step above fan fiction which  again